  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "Sale & Purchase: The second-hand market stayed busy, with a further thirty-one vessels reported sold (20 \nbulkers, 7 tankers and 4 containerships), on top of the deals covered in last week's report. \nDry bulk again dominated, with buyers active across the size range from Capesize down to Handysize and \nvalues holding firm. Tanker activity was quieter, with four MR newbuilding resales and the MR pair IDI and \nKALINKA the main priced deals. \nIn the bulker sector, 20 vessels changed hands: 3 Capesizes, 6 Post-Panamaxes, 2 Ultramaxes, 4 \nSupramaxes and 5 Handysizes.  \nFollowing last week's sale of the EVER ABILITY at USD 39.7 mill, Everest Shipping also sold her sister EVER \nBEAUTY (180,156 dwt, 2011 Qingdao Beihai/China, scrubber fitted) at high USD 39 mill to Chinese buyers, \nbasis delivery January - April 2027, confirming the level for older Chinese-built Capesizes. The one-year-\nyounger ASL OTSL 2 (180,643 dwt, 2012 Tsuneishi Cebu/Philippines) is understood to have achieved USD 48 \nmill, a premium of some USD 8 mill over the Qingdao Beihai-built sisters. Elsewhere in the segment, the \nJapanese-built FRIENDSHIP (abt 177,000 dwt, 2009 Namura/Japan) is understood to have been sold for \nUSD 33 mill. \nThe Post-Panamax segment was the busiest. Clients of Hayfin acquired the Oshima-built trio HARVEST \nTIME, HARVEST RAIN and HARVEST FROST (95,263 dwt each, 2014 - 2015 Oshima/Japan) en bloc for USD \n88 mill, or about USD 29.3 mill each. The Chinese-built NEW HALO (87,450 dwt, 2011 Hudong-\nZhonghua/China) fetched USD 16 mill, while the veteran CORONA JOYFUL (88,233 dwt, 2003 \nImabari/Japan) achieved a firm USD 11.8 mill. The MARINA (abt 87,000 dwt, 2006 IHI/Japan) is understood \nto have been sold for USD 14.9 mill. \nIn the geared sizes, the modern Japanese Ultramax SIDER MADEIRA (64,061 dwt, 2020 Mitsui/Japan) \ncommanded USD 37.4 mill, while Middle Eastern buyers paid USD 22.8 mill for the ISEACO GRACE (61,683 \ndwt, 2011 Oshima/Japan, SS/DD due 10/2027). \nAmong the Supramaxes, the SUPER RICE (abt 56,000 dwt, 2013 Jiangsu Hantong/China, scrubber ftd) is \nunderstood to be committed to Greek buyers at a price in the region of low-mid USD 19 mill, while the one-\nyear older JIN BI (56,361 dwt, 2012 Jiangsu New Hantong/China, SS/DD due 09/2027) achieved USD 17.8 \nmill. The Indonesian flagged CHLOE (56,770 dwt, 2010 Jiangsu Hantong/China) got a discounted USD 12 \nmill, most likely due to her Indonesian inter-island coal trade. The discount is evident when compared with \nthe same-age GANNET BULKER (abt 57,000 dwt, 2010 Yangzhou Dayang/China, scrubber ftd), which is \nunderstood to have been sold for USD 16.2 mill. \nContrary to our report last week, the AFRICA NOVA (56,781 dwt, 2012 Zhejiang Zengzhou/China, SS/DD \ndue 06/2027, BWTS fitted) has not been sold. She is inspectable at Walvis Bay before proceeding to Vizag, \nIndia, to discharge, and her sellers are asking mid-high USD 13 mill to test the market, basis delivery \nSingapore in January 2027. \nIn the Handysize sector, European buyers paid USD 22 mill for the open-hatch GREAT INTELLIGENCE \n(38,797 dwt, 2017 Guangzhou Wenchong/China, SS/DD due 12/2027). The CHRISTINA (32,573 dwt, 2011 \nJiangmen Nanyang/China) achieved USD 11.55 mill, about USD 2 mill above the USD 9.5 mill paid in July for \nher same-age, same-yard sister SEAMEC GALLANT (32,289 dwt, 2011 Jiangmen Nanyang/China). \nMiddle Eastern buyers paid USD 8 mill for the logs-fitted PARANA WARRIOR (28,415 dwt, 2007 \nShimanami/Japan).  \nOn the tanker front, 7 new sales were reported, while the price emerged for the coiled Suezmax JASMINE \nKNUTSEN (148,706 dwt, 2005 Samsung/Korea), reported last week as sold at an undisclosed price. She is \nnow understood to have been sold for USD 36.5 mill, against the USD 33 mill her sellers MISC paid in 3Q \n2025. \nIn the MR segment, UK buyers acquired four 50,000 dwt newbuilding resales at Qidong Qianyao (hulls \nYZJF2024-013 to -016, delivery November 2027 - November 2028) en bloc for USD 50 mill each. The MR \npair IDI (abt 46,000 dwt, 2007 Hyundai Mipo/Korea) and KALINKA (abt 47,000 dwt, 2008 Onomichi/Japan) \nis understood to have been sold en bloc for USD 51 mill. \nIn the container sector, the pair of 2,340 TEU newbuilding resales JIANGSU HUATAI HT-290 and HT-291 \n(delivery April and July 2027) were resold for USD 36 mill each. In addition, the NORDMAAS (abt 1,800 TEU, \n2019 Jiangsu/China) is understood to have been sold for USD 35.4 mill and the veteran AS FREYA (abt 1,200 \nTEU, 2004 Peene Werft/Germany) for USD 12 mill. \nFor a detailed review and additional information regarding this week's transactions, kindly refer to our \n\"representative sales\" section.",
    "demolition": "Recycling activity remained thin, with only one priced sale reported, as freight earnings stay \nattractive across all sectors and conventional candidates remain scarce.  \nThe only priced deal was the 1974-built mining vessel CORA (7,065 ldt), sold to India at USD 548 per LT, a \nlevel that reflects her specialised type rather than the general market.  \nPrice indications held broadly steady, but sentiment cooled. Bangladesh remains the best-paying \ndestination, although political unrest, weaker steel plate prices and sluggish domestic demand have made \nrecyclers more cautious.  \nIndian recyclers eased their offers by USD 20 per LT on a slow domestic steel market, although the coming \nmonths are traditionally the busiest for Indian steel consumption.  \nPakistan stayed steady, with buying capacity limited as only eleven yards hold DASR approval.  \nTurkey was unchanged, with production at a major Izmir steel mill still disrupted following last week's \naccident. \nFor an overview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer below:"
  },
